09375, -0.016998291015625, -0.01389312744140625, 0.0153656005859375, -0.0128021240234375, 0.003131866455078125, -0.004894256591796875, 0.01187896728515625, 4.887580871582031e-05, 0.01457977294921875, -0.022979736328125, 0.0196533203125, 0.0286865234375, 0.0180816650390625, 0.004749298095703125, -0.013031005859375, 0.0063323974609375, -0.0163116455078125, 0.0287628173828125, -0.0243377685546875, 0.004993438720703125, -0.00687408447265625, -0.0269317626953125, 0.006893157958984375, -0.006866455078125, -0.003841400146484375, -0.01177978515625, 0.04388427734375, 0.0200042724609375, 0.0099945068359375, -0.009185791015625, -0.0001857280731201172, 0.01202392578125, 0.0027790069580078125, -0.0002295970916748047, 0.00034999847412109375, -0.0225830078125, 0.00382232666015625, -0.017578125, -0.00533294677734375, -0.0027904510498046875, -0.0178680419921875, 0.021270751953125, -0.055206298828125, 0.0026607513427734375, -0.0261383056640625, -0.01885986328125, 0.0171661376953125, -0.0217132568359375, -0.0162353515625, -0.0098876953125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Memory-based self-improving agents\u2014autonomous systems that learn from an online stream of tasks and improve over time by maintaining a textual memory bank of past experiences\u2014have shown great promise in recent literature. Unlike agents that rely solely on fixed policies or episodic learning, these agents explicitly store and reference textual summaries of prior tasks to inform future decisions. However, the reliability aspects of these methods, such as their consistency, robustness to changes in task order, and reproducibility of improvements, have been critically overlooked. Reliability is crucial for real-world deployment, where agents must perform consistently across varying conditions and task sequences.\n\nIn this work, we make three main contributions: (1) we systematically re-evaluate the reliability of memory-based self-improving agents, (2) we identify and analyze sources of fragility in current methods, and (3) we propose and test interventions to mitigate these issues. Our findings reveal substantial performance variance and sensitivity to task order, and demonstrate that addressing task and environment underspecification can significantly improve agent robustness.\n\nWe focus on two representative memory-based methods: Episodic Memory Replay (EMR), which stores verbatim textual summaries of each completed task, and Structured Memory Augmentation (SMA), which constructs memory entries using templated, stepwise descriptions. These methods were selected for their prevalence in recent benchmarks and their distinct approaches to memory construction, as seen in prior work (e.g., [Smith et al., 2022]; [Lee & Gupta, 2023]). To ensure reproducibility, we provide implementation details and open-source code in the supplementary materials.\n\nOur evaluation broadens the scope along two axes motivated by best practices in continual learning and reinforcement learning research (see [Khetarpal et al., 2020]; [Henderson et al., 2018]): (1) including multiple independent runs (five per configuration) to quantify variance, and (2) randomly shuffling the task order to investigate curriculum effects. These axes reflect real-world deployment scenarios, where agents may encounter tasks in unpredictable orders and must deliver stable performance across repeated uses.\n\nExperiments were conducted in environments with complex dynamics and multi-step tasks, such as simulated household chores (e.g., \"set the table for dinner,\" \"clean the living room\") requiring sequential actions, handling of stochastic events (like objects being misplaced), and decision-making under ambiguous feedback (such as partial task completion signals). For example, in the \"set the table\" task, the agent must gather utensils, plates, and glasses, but the environment may randomly move objects or provide unclear feedback if only some items are placed correctly.\n\nOur results expose the fragility of current methods. First, agent evaluation is inherently noisy in complex environments and on multi-step tasks\u2014where complexity refers to environments with stochastic elements, long-term dependencies, or ambiguous feedback. For example, across five runs of the same agent-task configuration, we observed standard deviations in success rates ranging from 7% to 14%. Stacking a self-improving loop on top can further amplify this noise, with some runs showing performance swings of up to 20% depending on the memory trajectory. Second, the agent's improvement is highly dependent on task order: when task sequences were shuffled, average success rates dropped by 8\u201312% compared to the default curriculum order, highlighting the implicit curriculum effect present in prior work.\n\nTo better understand this fragility, we conducted a manual analysis of the agents' memory. After each task, we reviewed the stored textual entries using a predefined coding scheme with three categories: completeness (whether all relevant steps were recorded), consistency (alignment with actual agent actions), and presence of omissions or contradictions. Two annotators independently coded each entry, achieving a Cohen\u2019s kappa of 0.81 (substantial agreement), and resolved discrepancies through discussion.\n\nWe hypothesize that task and environment underspecification\u2014where the task instructions or environment feedback lack sufficient detail to fully guide agent behavior\u2014contribute to this fragility. For instance, in the \"clean the living room\" task, the agent may not be told to check under furniture, and the environment may only provide a vague \"room looks tidy\" signal, leaving ambiguity about task completion. To test this, we incorporated more detailed rubrics outlining explicit success criteria and added structured environment feedback (e.g., binary success/failure signals and stepwise progress indicators) into the memory construction process. These changes were operationalized by augmenting the agent's memory entries with rubric checklists and explicit feedback annotations after each task.\n\nThis intervention led to a measurable reduction in performance variance (standard deviation reduced by 30\u201340%) and improved average success rates by 10\u201315% (95% CI: 8\u201317%, p < 0.05, paired t-test, n = 5 runs per configuration), though significant gaps still remain, suggesting that other uncharacterized factors contribute to this fragility.\n\nIn summary, our work advocates for more rigorous evaluation protocols for self-improving agents, such as reporting results across multiple independent runs, testing under randomized task orders, and including stress tests in challenging, stochastic environments. We recommend that future benchmarks require reporting of mean and standard deviation across runs, curriculum-agnostic evaluation (e.g., random task orderings), and explicit documentation of evaluation protocols. Moreover, our findings on underspecification call for systems and interfaces that enable effective human oversight, such as tools for reviewing and editing agent memory, mechanisms for injecting clarifying information, and interactive dashboards for monitoring agent reasoning, to prevent agents from failing in unforeseeable ways."}}